00:12:38.801 --> 00:12:51.880
6 Chapter 3 No Fridge I was shocked.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:58.564
Uncle Fraser lived in a thatched white craft near the sea and surrounded by grass.

00:12:58.624 --> 00:13:08.095
white heather cottage had a tiny wooden front door and Uncle Fraser had to bend his head to go in and out.

00:13:08.155 --> 00:13:10.996
There were three rooms downstairs.

00:13:11.055 --> 00:13:14.527
Inside the craft it was cozy and warm.

00:13:14.587 --> 00:13:20.538
The kitchen was at the far end and there was a table near the window.

00:13:20.598 --> 00:13:25.390
A small fire burned with cooking pans hanging above it.

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:29.376
I looked out at the bright orange sky.

00:13:29.436 --> 00:13:32.464
We have great sunsets here."

00:13:32.524 --> 00:13:34.514
said Uncle Fraser.

00:13:34.574 --> 00:13:36.916
He showed me his study.

00:13:36.976 --> 00:13:41.578
He was a writer, and he wrote historical novels.

00:13:41.638 --> 00:13:47.502
There were pens and paper all over his desk and books everywhere.

00:13:47.562 --> 00:13:53.284
Crofts don't usually have an upstairs, but this one does.

00:13:53.344 --> 00:13:58.421
He said, he took me upstairs to show me my room.

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.436
It was lovely.

00:14:00.496 --> 00:14:05.439
There was a wooden bed and a red rug on the floor.

00:14:05.499 --> 00:14:11.863
Next to the bed there was a chest which looked like a pirate's treasure chest.

00:14:11.924 --> 00:14:17.878
There was a small desk near the window and a vase of fresh white heather.

00:14:17.938 --> 00:14:23.282
There were pictures of animals and wildflowers hanging on the walls.

00:14:23.342 --> 00:14:34.132
Uncle Fraser's bedroom was at the end of the corridor, and there was a small bathroom with a bathtub and a pile of soft white towels.

00:14:34.192 --> 00:14:38.576
It's very pretty, I said to Uncle Fraser.

00:14:38.636 --> 00:14:41.198
But do you get lonely here?

00:14:41.258 --> 00:14:47.043
Oh no, I have friends who live in the village, and I have my animals.

00:14:47.103 --> 00:14:50.607
We went downstairs and had dinner.

00:14:50.667 --> 00:14:56.040
There was homemade bread, creamy butter and goat cheese.

00:14:56.100 --> 00:15:00.082
For pudding, we had wild raspberries and cream.

00:15:00.142 --> 00:15:02.932
It was a delicious meal.

00:15:02.992 --> 00:15:07.454
It was getting dark and I looked for the light switch.

00:15:07.514 --> 00:15:10.726
Uncle Fraser saw me and smiled.

00:15:10.786 --> 00:15:12.366
Sorry, Megan.

00:15:12.426 --> 00:15:14.942
There is no electricity at the craft.

00:15:15.002 --> 00:15:16.990
No electricity.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.682
So you don't have a dishwasher?

00:15:19.742 --> 00:15:22.364
I said, looking at the plates.

00:15:22.424 --> 00:15:25.746
No, I'm the dishwasher.

00:15:25.806 --> 00:15:28.618
Said my uncle laughing.

00:15:28.678 --> 00:15:32.772
And you probably don't have a computer, I said.

00:15:32.832 --> 00:15:35.664
No, and I don't have a television.

00:15:35.724 --> 00:15:38.722
On Sky we create our own entertainment.

00:15:38.782 --> 00:15:45.602
I play the piano after dinner when friends come round, or I read by the light of candles and oil lamps.

00:15:45.662 --> 00:15:52.707
There are lots of board games in the corner, so we can play chess, drafts, monopoly or scrabble.

00:15:52.767 --> 00:15:54.078
Piano?

00:15:54.138 --> 00:15:55.599
Board games?

00:15:55.659 --> 00:15:57.870
I couldn't believe it.

00:15:57.930 --> 00:16:00.242
No electricity.

00:16:00.302 --> 00:16:04.856
I wanted to go home back to my technological world.

00:16:04.916 --> 00:16:08.621
So, where is the fridge Uncle Fraser?

00:16:08.681 --> 00:16:10.624
I don't need one.

00:16:10.684 --> 00:16:15.336
There is a cold cellar in this craft, and I grow most of my food.

00:16:15.396 --> 00:16:22.938
I milk the cows and goats early in the morning, so there is fresh milk every day, and I make my own cheese.

00:16:22.998 --> 00:16:25.650
I buy meat from the butchers when I want it.

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:28.230
It's a perfect life, Meghan.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:29.581
Perfect.

00:16:29.641 --> 00:16:38.227
I said to myself, as I climb the stairs to bed, I went into my bedroom and put the candle on my desk.

00:16:38.287 --> 00:16:40.719
I looked out of my window.

00:16:40.778 --> 00:16:45.430
The moon was bright, and there were so many stars.

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:53.085
And standing in the heather, stood a silvery white horse.

00:16:53.145 --> 00:17:03.675
Chapter 4 Dolly I woke up, but there was something wrong.

00:17:03.735 --> 00:17:06.132
Then I knew what it was.

00:17:06.191 --> 00:17:08.432
It was the silence.

00:17:08.493 --> 00:17:12.145
There was no sound of cars or traffic.

00:17:12.205 --> 00:17:13.736
Nothing.

00:17:13.797 --> 00:17:17.078
I opened my window and listened.

00:17:17.138 --> 00:17:22.241
And then I heard a bird sing and a sheep call under my window.

00:17:22.302 --> 00:17:25.174
I dressed quickly and went downstairs.

00:17:25.234 --> 00:17:28.385
A big cat sat in front of the fire.

00:17:28.446 --> 00:17:33.920
It looked like a tiger with its red, white and grey stripes.

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:36.751
My uncle was in the kitchen.

00:17:36.811 --> 00:17:38.822
Good morning, Megan.

00:17:38.882 --> 00:17:40.572
Did you sleep well?

00:17:40.633 --> 00:17:41.993
Yes, I did.

00:17:42.053 --> 00:17:45.004
I slept very well, I said.

00:17:45.064 --> 00:17:50.657
And I saw the silvery white horse again, Uncle Fraser, before I went to sleep.

00:17:50.717 --> 00:17:53.028
My uncle looked surprised.

00:17:53.088 --> 00:17:57.260
But he said nothing, and put two bowls on the table.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:02.777
Why do you look surprised, Uncle Fraser, when I tell you about the white horse?

00:18:02.837 --> 00:18:05.422
Uncle Fraser sat down.

00:18:05.482 --> 00:18:10.114
There is a legend, Megan, of a silvery white horse.

00:18:10.174 --> 00:18:12.915
Some people say it is a unicorn.

00:18:12.975 --> 00:18:14.815
A unicorn?

00:18:14.875 --> 00:18:16.156
I said.

00:18:16.216 --> 00:18:19.486
Maybe the horse I saw was a unicorn.

00:18:19.546 --> 00:18:23.348
It was a long way away, and it was difficult to see.

00:18:23.408 --> 00:18:24.988
What does the legend say?

00:18:25.008 --> 00:18:27.389
I don't know.

00:18:27.449 --> 00:18:31.888
Uncle Fraser put a big bowl of porridge on the table.

00:18:31.948 --> 00:18:37.638
A shepherd saw the unicorn, but it was a hundred years ago now.

00:18:37.698 --> 00:18:40.830
This was exciting news.

00:18:40.890 --> 00:18:44.142
Who can tell us about the unicorn?

00:18:44.202 --> 00:18:47.774
My uncle poured me a glass of fresh milk.

00:18:47.835 --> 00:18:51.086
Old Mrs. McTargety knows the legend.

00:18:51.146 --> 00:18:54.788
She's the old woman who lives on the other side of the village.

00:18:54.848 --> 00:18:56.700
We can ask her.

00:18:56.760 --> 00:19:00.798
Now eat your breakfast and know more questions.

00:19:00.858 --> 00:19:05.998
The porridge was delicious, and I ate it with cream and sugar.

00:19:06.058 --> 00:19:09.160
The cat came and sat next to me.

00:19:09.220 --> 00:19:11.502
This is Marmaduke.

00:19:11.562 --> 00:19:16.144
You didn't see him last night, because he likes to catch mice in the barn.

00:19:16.204 --> 00:19:17.866
Said my uncle.

00:19:17.926 --> 00:19:20.096
Let's go outside, Megan.

00:19:20.156 --> 00:19:22.608
I have a surprise for you.

00:19:22.668 --> 00:19:26.110
Uncle Fraser showed me his vegetable garden.

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:30.595
There was spinach, carrots, potatoes, and leeks.

00:19:30.655 --> 00:19:33.598
and there were rabbits trying to eat the lettuce.

00:19:33.658 --> 00:19:39.050
Uncle Fraser chased them away and they jumped over the dry stone walls.

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:46.584
Uncle Fraser had a field of sheep, four cows, three goats and a lot of chickens.

00:19:46.644 --> 00:19:52.687
He showed me how to milk the cows and it looked easy when he did it.

00:19:52.747 --> 00:19:55.828
The milk squirted into the bucket.

00:19:55.888 --> 00:19:59.782
But when I tried, nothing came out.

00:19:59.842 --> 00:20:04.298
Oh Uncle Fraser, I can't do it, I said.

00:20:04.358 --> 00:20:06.960
The animals don't know you, Megan.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:08.481
Said my uncle.

00:20:08.541 --> 00:20:10.472
He took my hands.

00:20:10.532 --> 00:20:13.324
And you need warm hands.

00:20:13.384 --> 00:20:17.608
Near the Croft, there was a small barn and stables.

00:20:17.668 --> 00:20:21.030
I looked inside the first stable.

00:20:21.090 --> 00:20:23.632
You met Darwin yesterday.

00:20:23.692 --> 00:20:27.546
Said my uncle, patting the beige pony's neck.

00:20:27.606 --> 00:20:29.928
He's my trap pony.

00:20:29.988 --> 00:20:35.529
In the second stable, there was a cream-coloured pony with black spots.

00:20:35.589 --> 00:20:38.250
This is Moon.

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.790
My neighbour's son, Ben, often rides him.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:43.801
I touched his nose.

00:20:43.861 --> 00:20:45.861
It was so soft.

00:20:45.921 --> 00:20:48.462
Why do you call him Moon?

00:20:48.522 --> 00:20:54.162
Because when you look at the Moon it is cream-coloured, but there are little black marks.

00:20:54.222 --> 00:20:56.962
I think he's the colour of the Moon.

00:20:57.022 --> 00:21:02.714
Uncle Fraser moved to the third stable, where there was the prettiest pony.

00:21:02.774 --> 00:21:08.280
It was beige with a blonde mane and tail, and had big dark eyes.

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:11.713
This is your pony, Meghan.

00:21:11.774 --> 00:21:16.138
And her name is Dolly.

00:21:16.198 --> 00:21:29.276
Chapter 5 The Arrival of Idris When I woke the next day, I felt so happy.

00:21:29.336 --> 00:21:35.548
I had a pony, a beautiful pony, and I wanted to write her.

00:21:35.608 --> 00:21:38.240
Then I thought of mum.

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:42.062
It was fun at the craft, but I missed her.

00:21:42.122 --> 00:21:45.052
I wrote her a text message.

00:21:45.112 --> 00:21:47.034
I'm fine.

00:21:47.094 --> 00:21:49.665
I've got a pony called Dolly.

00:21:49.725 --> 00:21:51.416
How are you?

00:21:51.476 --> 00:21:54.984
Lots and lots of love, Megan.

00:21:55.044 --> 00:22:00.286
I pushed send and waited.

00:22:00.346 --> 00:22:04.728
I'm not too bad, my darling.

00:22:04.788 --> 00:22:06.490
How lovely.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:08.090
A pony.

00:22:08.150 --> 00:22:09.962
Lots of love.

00:22:10.022 --> 00:22:11.763
Mum.

00:22:11.823 --> 00:22:14.786
I typed another message.

00:22:14.846 --> 00:22:16.887
Get better quickly.

00:22:16.947 --> 00:22:19.408
I want to bring you here.

00:22:19.468 --> 00:22:23.338
A message came back.

00:22:23.398 --> 00:22:26.558
Okay.

00:22:26.618 --> 00:22:29.770
I could hear voices downstairs.

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:34.432
One was Uncle Fraser's, but there was someone with him.

00:22:34.492 --> 00:22:36.842
I went into the sitting room.

00:22:36.902 --> 00:22:39.204
A boy stood near the fire.

00:22:39.264 --> 00:22:48.008
He was about my age, but he was very tall, and he had wavy blonde hair and pale blue eyes.

00:22:48.068 --> 00:22:54.778
He was wearing jeans and a checked shirt, and he had a box in his hands.

00:22:54.838 --> 00:22:58.608
Megan, this is Ben, our neighbour.

00:22:58.668 --> 00:23:02.862
My uncle said, Hi, I said.

00:23:02.922 --> 00:23:04.554
Hi Megan.

00:23:04.614 --> 00:23:06.985
Ben gave me the box.

00:23:07.045 --> 00:23:08.736
I found this.

00:23:08.797 --> 00:23:11.788
He said, as he opened it.

00:23:11.848 --> 00:23:14.741
We can look after it together.

00:23:14.801 --> 00:23:19.134
Inside the box was a baby otter.

00:23:19.194 --> 00:23:21.276
It looked at me.

00:23:21.336 --> 00:23:23.708
It was so sweet.

00:23:23.768 --> 00:23:26.158
Its name is Idris.

00:23:26.218 --> 00:23:27.580
Then said.

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:29.971
And I think it's hungry.

00:23:30.032 --> 00:23:34.695
Uncle Fraser knew what baby otters like for breakfast.

00:23:34.756 --> 00:23:40.110
He put some homemade bread in a bowl and poured milk on it.

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:43.854
Then he added a little fish oil.

00:23:43.914 --> 00:23:46.386
Idris loved it.

00:23:46.446 --> 00:23:50.883
He ate it all and then licked the plate.

00:23:50.943 --> 00:23:54.678
I think he wants some more, I said.

00:23:54.738 --> 00:23:57.950
Babies have lots of small meals.

00:23:58.010 --> 00:23:59.620
My uncle said.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:01.301
He needs a bed.

00:24:01.361 --> 00:24:05.082
You can find a box and some store in the barn.

00:24:05.142 --> 00:24:12.766
Sir Ben and I made Idris a bed and put it in a corner of the sitting-room near the fire.

00:24:12.826 --> 00:24:19.944
We introduced him to Marmaduke, who was not very pleased to see another animal in the house.

00:24:20.004 --> 00:24:25.753
Then, after lunch, my uncle appeared with the pony and trap.

00:24:25.813 --> 00:24:27.864
Come on, you two.

00:24:27.924 --> 00:24:32.707
We have to buy Meg and some riding trousers, boots, and a jacket.

00:24:32.767 --> 00:24:39.481
We are in the highlands of Scotland, and sometimes mists come down, and it can get very cold.

00:24:39.542 --> 00:24:44.134
We drove into Portree, the main town on Skye.

00:24:44.194 --> 00:24:48.026
Portree in Gaelic means Kingsport.

00:24:48.086 --> 00:24:49.560
Said Ben.

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:53.536
We went to the harbour and watched the boats.

00:24:53.596 --> 00:24:59.269
In Wentworth Street, my uncle bought the clothes I needed.

00:24:59.330 --> 00:25:06.183
Then we went to the post office, and I sent mum a postcard with a highland cow on it.

00:25:06.243 --> 00:25:13.468
Then had dinner with us in the craft, and after dinner Uncle Fraser played the piano.

00:25:13.528 --> 00:25:17.088
We sang hymns and old songs.

00:25:17.148 --> 00:25:21.872
Then we all sang the Skye boat song.

00:25:21.932 --> 00:25:27.766
Speedbunny boat like a bird on the wing.

00:25:27.786 --> 00:25:32.489
Over the sailors crying.

00:25:32.549 --> 00:25:37.593
Carry the lad that's born to be king.

00:25:37.613 --> 00:25:42.426
Over the sea to Skye.

00:25:42.486 --> 00:25:48.914
Idris and Mamadook thought we were very strange.

00:25:48.974 --> 00:25:55.950
clans, tartan and kilts.

00:25:56.010 --> 00:25:58.700
Clans.

00:25:58.760 --> 00:26:03.741
Clan is a Gaelic word that means family.

00:26:03.801 --> 00:26:12.104
The great clans of the 16th and 17th centuries were large families ruled by chiefs.

00:26:12.164 --> 00:26:18.430
People could distinguish the clans from the leaves they wore in their hats.

00:26:18.490 --> 00:26:25.870
the McDonalds wore Heather, and the MacIntoshies wore a piece of Holly.

00:26:25.931 --> 00:26:38.520
For many years, the King or Queen of Britain did not have any authority over the Highlands, and there were many battles between the different families.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:48.906
Tartan Each clan had its own tartan, and only members of that clan could wear it.

00:26:48.966 --> 00:26:52.108
So, what is tartan?

00:26:52.168 --> 00:26:57.112
It is a wool material with stripes of different colours.

00:26:57.172 --> 00:27:04.279
In the past, in every area of the Highlands, there was a man who made the tartan.

00:27:04.338 --> 00:27:08.082
He used local plants for the colours.

00:27:08.142 --> 00:27:15.818
Families who lived on the coasts had flesh colours in their tartans from the seaweed dye.

00:27:15.878 --> 00:27:27.680
Families who lived in the hills had yellows, deep greens and brownish orange which came from the heathers which grew on the hills.

00:27:27.740 --> 00:27:34.872
Berries gave a purple colour and leaves gave different shades of green.

00:27:34.932 --> 00:27:41.539
King James V was the first person to buy tartan for his wife to wear.

00:27:41.599 --> 00:27:50.740
There are tartans for all the famous Scottish names, like Campbell, MacLeod and McDonald.

00:27:50.800 --> 00:28:02.365
Kiltz The kilt is the national dress of Scotland, and for many Scottish people it is a symbol of honour.

00:28:02.425 --> 00:28:12.327
In the past it was a kind of uniform and distinguished the many clans, particularly when they went into battle.

00:28:12.387 --> 00:28:19.746
The kilt, which is a skirt made of tartan, started many years ago as a plaid.

00:28:19.806 --> 00:28:30.730
It was a piece of material, twelve to fifteen feet long, and the highlanders put it round their waist in folds.

00:28:30.790 --> 00:28:36.361
It was heavy, and it protected the soldiers like armour.

00:28:36.421 --> 00:28:43.292
When the knights became cold, they used it as a blanket to keep them warm.

00:28:43.352 --> 00:28:54.936
Scottish people still wear kilts today with a sporen, which is a bag of fur or leather and knee-high socks.

00:28:54.996 --> 00:29:02.528
They wear the kilts to weddings and when they go to the Highland Games.

00:29:02.588 --> 00:29:13.632
CHAPTER 6 Scottish Mists Ben came to the croft the next morning.

00:29:13.692 --> 00:29:22.834
He gave me a writing lesson, and then we decided to take Idris out for a walk and pick wild raspberries for tea.

00:29:22.894 --> 00:29:25.545
Don't forget your jacket.

00:29:25.605 --> 00:29:27.426
So, Uncle Fraser.

00:29:27.486 --> 00:29:30.127
The weather looks uncertain.

00:29:30.187 --> 00:29:32.227
And don't go far.

00:29:32.287 --> 00:29:35.310
Okay, we both said.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:38.504
Idris was happy to go out with us.

00:29:38.564 --> 00:29:46.139
He ran and jumped among the world flowers and when we reached the small loch he swam.

00:29:46.199 --> 00:29:51.722
Otters are playful animals and Idris made a mud shoot.

00:29:51.782 --> 00:29:57.486
He was very happy because now he could slide into the water.

00:29:57.546 --> 00:30:02.952
Bell and I sat in the warm sunshine and watched him.

00:30:03.012 --> 00:30:06.708
Why did Uncle Fraser tell me to bring a jacket?

00:30:06.768 --> 00:30:08.208
I said to Ben.

00:30:08.268 --> 00:30:10.160
The weather's hot.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:12.342
Ben smiled.

00:30:12.402 --> 00:30:17.456
The weather's worn now, but it can change very quickly.

00:30:17.516 --> 00:30:20.809
We decided to pick some raspberries.

00:30:20.869 --> 00:30:25.672
There were some growing near a small wood, and we picked lots.

00:30:25.732 --> 00:30:32.072
Then we saw black currents, so we went into the wood, and either is followed us.

00:30:32.132 --> 00:30:35.758
The black currents were big and delicious.

00:30:35.818 --> 00:30:38.772
Did I tell you I saw a unicorn?

00:30:38.832 --> 00:30:45.558
I said to Ben, as we walked along a little path, Ben laughed.

00:30:45.618 --> 00:30:49.072
Unicorns don't exist.

00:30:49.133 --> 00:30:51.476
That's what I thought.

00:30:51.536 --> 00:30:56.510
But Uncle Fraser said that a shepherd saw one here a hundred years ago.

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:58.492
There's a legend, he says.

00:30:58.552 --> 00:31:03.526
But we need to speak to old Mrs. McTagity.

00:31:03.586 --> 00:31:06.508
Ben turned and looked at me.

00:31:06.568 --> 00:31:09.882
He want to speak to the Lord's McTagity.

00:31:09.942 --> 00:31:12.514
Do you know who she is?

00:31:12.574 --> 00:31:14.836
No, I said.

00:31:14.896 --> 00:31:17.572
Eating some more black currents.

00:31:17.632 --> 00:31:20.806
We went down another path.

00:31:20.866 --> 00:31:24.016
Some folks say she's a witch.

00:31:24.076 --> 00:31:26.577
She lives in the Black House.

00:31:26.637 --> 00:31:30.298
It's the croft on the headland down by the beach.

00:31:30.358 --> 00:31:33.559
She doesn't come to the village very often.

00:31:33.619 --> 00:31:39.220
Mr. Mackie, who owns the grosses in the village, takes her food.

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:42.671
Why do the villagers think she's a witch?

00:31:42.731 --> 00:31:43.895
I asked.

00:31:43.956 --> 00:31:46.822
because the wild animals come to her.

00:31:46.882 --> 00:31:50.114
Eagles fly down and sit on her hand.

00:31:50.174 --> 00:31:55.278
The red deer come to her door for food and so do the seals.

00:31:55.338 --> 00:31:59.350
That doesn't mean that she is a witch, I said.

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:02.422
Well, you can't go to her house alone.

00:32:02.482 --> 00:32:03.933
I'll come with you.

00:32:03.993 --> 00:32:09.356
Ben said, and then stopped and looked up at the sky.

00:32:09.416 --> 00:32:14.424
The cold wind blew and the blue sky was now grey.

00:32:14.484 --> 00:32:16.099
We must go back."

00:32:16.159 --> 00:32:20.402
said Ben, and he put eitheris in his pocket.

00:32:20.462 --> 00:32:24.356
We turned round, but the mist came down.

00:32:24.416 --> 00:32:30.480
The white mist swirled all around, and we couldn't see the path in front of us.

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:32.212
We were lost.

00:32:32.272 --> 00:32:35.615
Walk slowly and hold my hand.

00:32:35.675 --> 00:32:37.116
Said Ben.

00:32:37.176 --> 00:32:41.620
It was very cold, and soon our hair was wet.

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:44.840
Thank goodness I had my jacket.

00:32:44.900 --> 00:32:49.260
Ben had a small torch and whistle tied on his jacket.

00:32:49.320 --> 00:32:52.832
But we were far from any houses and crafts.

00:32:52.893 --> 00:32:55.544
No one heard our calls.

00:32:55.604 --> 00:33:00.068
We walked on until suddenly there was a noise.

00:33:00.128 --> 00:33:03.220
It came from behind a tall tree.

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:04.580
What was that?

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:07.683
Cried Ben, shining his torch.

00:33:07.743 --> 00:33:09.770
But I knew.

00:33:09.830 --> 00:33:17.818
Just in front of us, in the circle of torchlight, stood the silvery white unicorn.

00:33:17.878 --> 00:33:22.879
It bowed its head and looked at me with its dark eyes.

00:33:22.939 --> 00:33:25.800
It called softly.

00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:29.330
It's the unicorn, I said to Ben.

00:33:29.390 --> 00:33:32.071
It wants to show us the way home.

00:33:32.131 --> 00:33:35.432
But then you don't believe in unicorns?

00:33:35.492 --> 00:33:37.412
Ben smiled.

00:33:37.472 --> 00:33:38.668
I didn't.

00:33:38.728 --> 00:33:40.314
But I do know.

00:33:40.374 --> 00:33:42.276
Let's follow it."

00:33:42.336 --> 00:33:48.912
We could see the silvery white unicorn clearly as we followed it through the wood.

00:33:48.972 --> 00:33:55.767
Slowly and patiently it led us across the fields to white heather cottage.

00:33:55.827 --> 00:34:02.702
The lights were on and as we walked through the gate the unicorn turned and looked at me.

00:34:02.762 --> 00:34:06.378
Then it galloped away across the heather.

00:34:06.438 --> 00:34:09.072
Uncle Fraser opened the door.

00:34:09.133 --> 00:34:11.344
His face was pale.

00:34:11.404 --> 00:34:16.178
Thank goodness you're home.

00:34:16.238 --> 00:34:21.132
Listening activity.

00:34:21.192 --> 00:34:25.145
The weather is warm and sunny this afternoon.

00:34:25.206 --> 00:34:29.068
But the forecast for early evening is uncertain.

00:34:29.128 --> 00:34:34.603
It could become windy and cool and the mist will come down.

00:34:34.664 --> 00:34:39.172
It will be very cold later.

00:34:39.232 --> 00:34:53.364
Chapter 7 Kendra Dolores McTagity lived in the Black House where the sea crashed and the seagulls flew.

00:34:53.424 --> 00:34:55.714
It was a wild place.

00:34:55.774 --> 00:34:59.486
No one went there, not even the fishermen.

00:34:59.546 --> 00:35:05.628
Uncle Fraser wanted to come with us, but we were determined to do this alone.

00:35:05.688 --> 00:35:11.116
He drove us to the grassy path which led to her croft.

00:35:11.176 --> 00:35:14.001
Take her some raspberry jam.

00:35:14.061 --> 00:35:16.052
Said Uncle Fraser.

00:35:16.112 --> 00:35:18.984
Tell her it's from me.

00:35:19.044 --> 00:35:23.206
Ben and I followed the path to her gate.

00:35:23.266 --> 00:35:27.659
But when we knocked on the front door, no one answered.

00:35:27.719 --> 00:35:31.070
Let's go round to the back, I said.

00:35:31.130 --> 00:35:33.572
Ben wasn't so sure.

00:35:33.632 --> 00:35:35.753
She may cast a spell.

00:35:35.813 --> 00:35:39.356
He said, but he came with me.

00:35:39.416 --> 00:35:42.808
The back of the house was beautiful.

00:35:42.868 --> 00:35:49.293
There was some grass, then black rocks, and then the white sandy beach.

00:35:49.353 --> 00:35:55.067
An old lady stood by the rocks, assured about her head.

00:35:55.128 --> 00:35:58.550
She was surrounded by animals.

00:35:58.610 --> 00:36:02.496
Red Rowan dear ate bread from her hands.

00:36:02.556 --> 00:36:10.752
Birds flew near her, and seal pups sat on the rocks calling to her with their husky voices.

00:36:10.812 --> 00:36:18.686
Near the grass, I saw puffins chattering to each other and red squirrels eating nuts.

00:36:18.746 --> 00:36:22.228
The old woman spoke to us.

00:36:22.288 --> 00:36:25.490
Stand quite still.

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:29.491
Now move forward slowly.

00:36:29.552 --> 00:36:31.792
We did as she asked.

00:36:31.852 --> 00:36:33.213
That's right.

00:36:33.273 --> 00:36:35.404
Now come here, Ladi.

00:36:35.464 --> 00:36:38.746
Then move to her right hand side.

00:36:38.806 --> 00:36:44.257
She caught a young eagle in her hand and she put it on his arm.

00:36:44.317 --> 00:36:46.927
The bird looked at him.

00:36:46.987 --> 00:36:48.808
Talk to him, Ladi.

00:36:48.868 --> 00:36:51.219
Tell him how beautiful he is.

00:36:51.279 --> 00:36:53.650
Said Dolores McTagity.

00:36:53.710 --> 00:36:55.421
Now you, Lassie.

00:36:55.481 --> 00:36:59.703
She said to me, I moved to her left side.

00:36:59.763 --> 00:37:02.604
You can give the deer some bread.

00:37:02.664 --> 00:37:07.184
The deer were not afraid because the old woman was there.

00:37:07.245 --> 00:37:09.904
They took the bread from my hand.

00:37:09.964 --> 00:37:11.944
That's all for today.

00:37:12.004 --> 00:37:15.366
Dolores McTagity said to the animals.

00:37:15.426 --> 00:37:18.056
The eagle flew into the air.

00:37:18.116 --> 00:37:23.628
The deer went off across the field, and the seals returned to the sea.

00:37:23.688 --> 00:37:27.049
Mrs. McTagity looked at us.

00:37:27.110 --> 00:37:31.492
Let's go in and have a nice cup of hot chocolate.

00:37:31.552 --> 00:37:39.196
The Black House was dark and oppressive from the outside, but inside it was light and cozy.

00:37:39.256 --> 00:37:43.660
Mrs. McTagity, here is some raspberry jam for you."

00:37:43.720 --> 00:37:45.992
I said, giving her the jar.

00:37:46.052 --> 00:37:48.873
It's from my Uncle Fraser.

00:37:48.933 --> 00:37:52.534
Dolores McTagity thanked me for the jam.

00:37:52.594 --> 00:37:57.105
But as she took it, she looked into my eyes.

00:37:57.165 --> 00:38:02.116
Oh, she said, the tea leaves.

00:38:02.176 --> 00:38:05.307
The tea leaves said a girl will come.

00:38:05.367 --> 00:38:09.058
She took my hand and we sat down.

00:38:09.118 --> 00:38:10.798
It's her eyes.

00:38:10.858 --> 00:38:15.719
She said to Ben, this girl's got magic in her eyes.

00:38:15.780 --> 00:38:17.090
Has she?

00:38:17.150 --> 00:38:18.530
Said Ben.

00:38:18.591 --> 00:38:22.052
We came to speak to you about the unicorn.

00:38:22.112 --> 00:38:26.123
Dolores McTagity stared at us.

00:38:26.183 --> 00:38:28.994
So it's through.

00:38:29.054 --> 00:38:30.534
She whispered.

00:38:30.594 --> 00:38:34.085
Kendra, the last unicorn is back.

00:38:34.145 --> 00:38:37.096
The seals told me he was here.

00:38:37.156 --> 00:38:40.757
Mrs McTagity, I said.

00:38:40.817 --> 00:38:45.368
The unicorn helped us the other day when the mist came down.

00:38:45.428 --> 00:38:48.077
It led us back to the house.

00:38:48.137 --> 00:38:51.828
Uncle Fraser said that you know the legend.

00:38:51.888 --> 00:38:55.070
Oh yes, I know the legend.

00:38:55.130 --> 00:39:00.524
It's your magic eyes that wore Kendra from his sleep and now he wants to help.

00:39:00.584 --> 00:39:05.598
The legend says that Kendra will come back when he is really needed.

00:39:05.658 --> 00:39:08.160
You need him, Lassie, don't you?

00:39:08.220 --> 00:39:09.860
I know it.

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:13.112
Yes, I said softly.

00:39:13.172 --> 00:39:19.678
Touch the unicorn, and you can have whatever wish your heart desires.

00:39:19.738 --> 00:39:26.263
Dolores McTagity took us to the door, and she brushed my face with her hand.

00:39:26.323 --> 00:39:31.638
Goulasi, and find Kendra!

00:39:31.698 --> 00:39:43.936
Chapter 8 The Full Moon There's a full moon tonight and the weather's warm.

00:39:43.996 --> 00:39:47.058
Let's have a barbecue on the beach.

00:39:47.118 --> 00:39:49.128
Said Uncle Fraser.

00:39:49.188 --> 00:39:52.760
That's a great idea, I said.

00:39:52.820 --> 00:39:56.152
We can collect wood and build a fire.

00:39:56.212 --> 00:39:59.473
I've got potatoes and hamburgers.

00:39:59.533 --> 00:40:02.734
I went to the butchers in portrait this morning.

00:40:02.794 --> 00:40:07.316
Uncle Fraser said as he went down into the cellar.

00:40:07.376 --> 00:40:10.070
I sent mum a text message.

00:40:10.130 --> 00:40:11.922
How are you?

00:40:11.982 --> 00:40:14.734
I love you, Megan.

00:40:14.794 --> 00:40:20.116
I waited, but she didn't reply, so I phoned.

00:40:20.176 --> 00:40:23.046
Mrs. Brown answered the phone.

00:40:23.106 --> 00:40:25.538
Her voice was sad.

00:40:25.598 --> 00:40:28.228
The doctor's here, Megan.

00:40:28.288 --> 00:40:30.850
Your mum's not well at all.

00:40:30.910 --> 00:40:33.230
I'll ring you later.

00:40:33.290 --> 00:40:41.080
I bit my lip, but the tears still fell, and I went outside to talk to Dolly.

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:46.010
I put my face in her mane until I felt better.

00:40:46.071 --> 00:40:49.293
Kendra, where are you?

00:40:49.353 --> 00:40:51.414
I said to the wind.

00:40:51.475 --> 00:40:59.199
Before the sunset, Ben, Uncle Fraser and I carried our baskets of food to the beach.

00:40:59.260 --> 00:41:03.842
We collected firewood and soon we had a big fire.

00:41:03.902 --> 00:41:08.548
The flames jumped and we had a wonderful barbecue.

00:41:08.608 --> 00:41:14.172
The moon came out full and bright, and we sang round the fire.

00:41:14.232 --> 00:41:16.754
Uncle Fraser wore his kilt.

00:41:16.814 --> 00:41:23.647
He brought his bagpipes and his accordion, and played us some bagpipe music first.

00:41:23.707 --> 00:41:25.898
He was really good.

00:41:25.958 --> 00:41:32.081
Then Uncle Fraser played his accordion, and Ben showed me some Scottish dancing.

00:41:32.141 --> 00:41:36.724
It was wonderful, and I laughed and clapped my hands.

00:41:36.784 --> 00:41:40.316
Then we sang all the songs we knew.

00:41:40.376 --> 00:41:49.240
It was when we started singing the Skyboat song that I saw something silvery white galloping towards us.

00:41:49.300 --> 00:41:50.911
Kendra!

00:41:50.971 --> 00:41:52.562
It's Kendra!

00:41:52.622 --> 00:41:53.922
I shouted.

00:41:53.982 --> 00:42:00.136
And I started to run towards the unicorn, my unicorn, the last unicorn.

00:42:00.196 --> 00:42:01.646
Wait for me!

00:42:01.706 --> 00:42:03.097
Shouted Ben.

00:42:03.157 --> 00:42:05.222
But I couldn't hear him.

00:42:05.282 --> 00:42:08.206
The unicorn was close now.

00:42:08.266 --> 00:42:13.340
I could see the horn in the middle of its forehead and its silvery mane.

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:17.634
And then it stopped and stood quite still.

00:42:17.694 --> 00:42:24.950
Its dark brown eyes looked into mine and it made a soft sound.

00:42:25.010 --> 00:42:29.757
Slowly I went up to the unicorn.

00:42:29.818 --> 00:42:36.976
I touched its velvet nose, brushed its neck and its silken mane.

00:42:37.036 --> 00:42:39.777
Make a wish.

00:42:39.838 --> 00:42:42.550
Came a thousand whispers.

00:42:42.610 --> 00:42:46.062
Make a wish.

00:42:46.122 --> 00:42:49.114
I thought of the bullies at school.

00:42:49.174 --> 00:42:56.197
I wanted them to leave me alone, but there was something far more important.

00:42:56.258 --> 00:43:01.882
I touched the unicorn's horn and made a wish.

00:43:01.942 --> 00:43:04.824
The moon shone brighter.

00:43:04.884 --> 00:43:07.586
And suddenly I was alone.

00:43:07.646 --> 00:43:10.348
Kendra was galloping away.

00:43:10.408 --> 00:43:11.409
Out.

00:43:11.470 --> 00:43:14.983
Out to see.

00:43:15.043 --> 00:43:19.746
Listening activity.

00:43:19.806 --> 00:43:22.999
Example.

00:43:23.059 --> 00:43:26.728
What time are we going to the beach?

00:43:26.788 --> 00:43:34.206
Well, it gets dark at about 10 o'clock, and we need to build our bonfire.

00:43:34.266 --> 00:43:38.698
People walking with their dogs go home at about 7 p.m.

00:43:38.758 --> 00:43:45.372
So we could be there at 7.30.

00:43:45.432 --> 00:43:52.566
We can cook potatoes in the fire and hamburgers in a pan on the fire.

00:43:52.626 --> 00:43:55.406
Have you got any chocolate cakes?

00:43:55.466 --> 00:43:59.337
No, I haven't, but there are strawberry cakes.

00:43:59.397 --> 00:44:01.008
I can bring those.

00:44:01.068 --> 00:44:02.149
Good.

00:44:02.209 --> 00:44:04.640
Have you got any orange juice?

00:44:04.700 --> 00:44:07.072
No, I've got lemonade.

00:44:07.132 --> 00:44:09.404
I can bring that.

00:44:09.464 --> 00:44:11.956
Two.

00:44:12.016 --> 00:44:17.028
Usually, the meat at the butchers is rarely expensive.

00:44:17.089 --> 00:44:24.862
Hamburgers usually cost about four pounds, but they are having a special barbecue sale.

00:44:24.922 --> 00:44:31.353
The hamburgers were two pounds fifty, so I was pleased.

00:44:31.414 --> 00:44:34.106
Three.

00:44:34.166 --> 00:44:40.970
I wanted to buy a newspaper, but they had sold out in the village shop.

00:44:41.030 --> 00:44:49.614
Duncan, who owns the pump, said I could have his, so I didn't look for newspapers in the gift shop.

00:44:49.674 --> 00:44:52.016
Four.

00:44:52.076 --> 00:44:55.159
Where shall we build the bonfire?

00:44:55.219 --> 00:45:00.542
We can't build it near the houses or near the trees.

00:45:00.602 --> 00:45:08.276
The best possession is between the rocks and the sea.

00:45:08.337 --> 00:45:12.968
Scottish music.

00:45:13.029 --> 00:45:19.092
Scottish music is unique and it is famous all over the world.

00:45:19.152 --> 00:45:25.866
In the past, the highland is used music, dancing and storytelling as entertainment.

00:45:25.926 --> 00:45:28.928
They made their own instruments.

00:45:28.988 --> 00:45:31.378
Baudrons.

00:45:31.438 --> 00:45:37.660
They are simple drums of goat skin pulled over a round wooden frame.

00:45:37.720 --> 00:45:46.633
They used them to accompany the highlanders into fierce battles, because the sound they made motivated the men.

00:45:46.693 --> 00:45:48.944
Bagpipes.

00:45:49.004 --> 00:45:53.634
Bagpipes are among the oldest musical instruments.

00:45:53.694 --> 00:45:59.827
The pipes, as they are often called, date back to 100 CE.

00:45:59.887 --> 00:46:04.069
Medieval bagpipes were made from natural materials.

00:46:04.129 --> 00:46:14.975
There was a windbag made of sheepskin, a wooden pipe fulfilling the bag with air, and a flute pipe which the pipe used to make the notes.

00:46:15.035 --> 00:46:19.350
The sound came through pipes attached to the bag.

00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:26.478
The bagpipes became popular during the 15th century, as musical instruments of war.

00:46:26.538 --> 00:46:37.168
Whenever a clan went into battle, the pipers accompanied the men and used their sound to raise the spirits of their clan and frightened the enemy.

00:46:37.228 --> 00:46:45.344
The sound of the pipes and the war cries of the kilted soldiers of the north frightened the hearts of English soldiers.

00:46:45.404 --> 00:46:56.808
After the Jacobite rising in the 18th century and the Battle of Cologne, the English banned bagpipes because they were considered an instrument of war.

00:46:56.868 --> 00:46:59.929
They also banned kilts.

00:46:59.989 --> 00:47:09.252
The Highland Fiddle A fiddle and a violin are identical, but the music they play is different.

00:47:09.312 --> 00:47:15.187
A violin plays classical music, and a fiddle plays folk music.

00:47:15.247 --> 00:47:21.022
The fiddle is used in the highlands to play reels for highland dancing.

00:47:21.082 --> 00:47:28.574
Faden In the Celtic world, whistles date back to the 11th century.

00:47:28.634 --> 00:47:37.646
Faden's were simple, six-hulled whistles, and they played them by blowing through a mouthpiece which was made of clay.

00:47:37.706 --> 00:47:47.574
The Gaelic harp The Gaelic harp is one of the harps to survive from the devil times and it was made on the west of Scotland.

00:47:47.634 --> 00:47:54.728
It had 32 strings, was made of wood and was about 95 centimetres high.

00:47:54.788 --> 00:48:00.652
It was an aristocratic instrument and was played before the chiefs of clans.

00:48:00.712 --> 00:48:07.154
Harp music was transmitted orally from teacher to student.

00:48:07.214 --> 00:48:14.355
It is pronounced, Cayley, and comes from a Gaelic word, which means visit.

00:48:14.415 --> 00:48:20.148
It is a social event, an evening of traditional Scottish dancing and music.

00:48:20.208 --> 00:48:32.458
In the highlands of Scotland, they are usually more concerts than dancing events.

00:48:32.518 --> 00:48:50.560
It was late and I had slept deeply and I felt happy, really happy for the first time in a long while.

00:48:50.620 --> 00:48:57.132
My mobile phone rang and I jumped out of bed.

00:48:57.192 --> 00:48:58.692
Where was it?

00:48:58.752 --> 00:49:01.774
I didn't use it very much at the craft.

00:49:01.834 --> 00:49:08.186
I had so many other things to do and phones and televisions were no longer important.

00:49:08.246 --> 00:49:13.310
I looked under my clothes and found it in the pocket of my jeans.

00:49:13.370 --> 00:49:14.800
Hello?

00:49:14.860 --> 00:49:17.572
Megan, it's Mrs. Brown.

00:49:17.632 --> 00:49:21.404
Dr. Barnes is here and he wants to speak to you.

00:49:21.464 --> 00:49:23.486
I felt cold.

00:49:23.546 --> 00:49:26.688
Did he want to tell me something terrible?

00:49:26.748 --> 00:49:28.700
Good morning, Megan.

00:49:28.760 --> 00:49:31.042
Came his loud voice.

00:49:31.102 --> 00:49:34.504
I have good news, great news.

00:49:34.564 --> 00:49:37.755
Yesterday evening your mother was very ill.

00:49:37.815 --> 00:49:39.984
I didn't know what to do.

00:49:40.044 --> 00:49:44.946
She had a high temperature and Mrs. Brown and I sat with her.

00:49:45.006 --> 00:49:52.657
Then, suddenly, at about midnight, she sat up in bed and said, I feel better.

00:49:52.717 --> 00:49:56.098
Could I have a cup of tea and a slice of toast, please?

00:49:56.158 --> 00:49:58.798
Her temperature was normal.

00:49:58.859 --> 00:50:00.569
It's a miracle, Megan.

00:50:00.629 --> 00:50:02.149
A miracle.

00:50:02.209 --> 00:50:05.948
I ran downstairs to tell Uncle Fraser.

00:50:06.008 --> 00:50:08.857
The unicorn, he gave me a wish.

00:50:08.917 --> 00:50:13.319
Mum's better and it was Kendra's wish that made her well.

00:50:13.379 --> 00:50:19.362
My uncle turned and walked over to the window and looked out at the sea.

00:50:19.422 --> 00:50:21.392
I left him alone.

00:50:21.452 --> 00:50:24.984
He didn't want me to see his tears of happiness.

00:50:25.044 --> 00:50:27.775
I went outside with Idris.

00:50:27.836 --> 00:50:30.776
Ben was there with Dolly and Moon.

00:50:30.837 --> 00:50:40.362
He helped me to get on Dolly and As we took the path that led down to the sea, I told him about Mum and Kendra's wish.

00:50:40.422 --> 00:50:45.035
What will you do about the bullies at school when you go back, Megan?

00:50:45.095 --> 00:50:46.466
He asked.

00:50:46.526 --> 00:50:52.149
I'll speak to Mum when she's completely better and to a teacher at school.

00:50:52.209 --> 00:50:54.100
It will be all right.

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:56.911
I want you to come and visit me in London.

00:50:56.972 --> 00:50:59.734
I'll miss you, I said.

00:50:59.794 --> 00:51:01.724
I'll miss you more.

00:51:01.784 --> 00:51:03.247
Said Ben.

00:51:03.307 --> 00:51:07.030
A month on Sky passed so quickly.

00:51:07.091 --> 00:51:09.132
I wanted to see Mum.

00:51:09.192 --> 00:51:14.794
But I didn't want to leave Ben, Uncle Fraser, the ponies and Idris.

00:51:14.855 --> 00:51:18.326
Ben and Uncle Fraser looked at each other.

00:51:18.386 --> 00:51:20.938
It was a secret look.

00:51:20.998 --> 00:51:25.800
Meghan, you can't take Idris or Dolly back home.

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:28.731
So we have a present for you.

00:51:28.791 --> 00:51:30.802
So, Uncle Fraser.

00:51:30.862 --> 00:51:33.492
It's outside in the trap.

00:51:33.552 --> 00:51:39.053
Dolores McTagity gave it to me, and she thinks you will be happy together.

00:51:39.112 --> 00:51:44.516
Sitting on my suitcase was a beautiful cock-a-spaniel puppy.

00:51:44.576 --> 00:51:48.028
Thank you, I said, and I hugged them.

00:51:48.088 --> 00:51:52.560
She's beautiful, but I don't want to leave you.

00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:57.623
We can write and speak on the phone, and you can come back soon.

00:51:57.683 --> 00:51:59.074
Said Ben.

00:51:59.134 --> 00:52:01.280
Come back with my sister.

00:52:01.341 --> 00:52:05.048
Uncle Fraser said, as he kissed my cheek.

00:52:05.108 --> 00:52:06.949
Come for new year.

00:52:07.009 --> 00:52:09.470
We call it Hogmanee in Scotland.

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:12.512
We have wonderful celebrations in the village.

00:52:12.572 --> 00:52:17.004
There is a custom here which brings good luck for the coming year.

00:52:17.064 --> 00:52:23.097
When the clock strikes midnight, some of us from the village go to Betty McAllister's house.

00:52:23.157 --> 00:52:27.500
We take a piece of bread, some money, and some coal.

00:52:27.560 --> 00:52:30.684
All these things bring good luck.

00:52:30.744 --> 00:52:34.140
The coal is so that the house is always warm.

00:52:34.200 --> 00:52:37.544
The bread is so there is always food to eat.

00:52:37.604 --> 00:52:40.836
And the money, so that there is always money.

00:52:40.897 --> 00:52:44.520
Then we throw ashes out of the back door.

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:46.672
Why do you do that?

00:52:46.732 --> 00:52:48.054
I asked.

00:52:48.114 --> 00:52:50.576
It's the old year leaving.

00:52:50.636 --> 00:52:53.007
Continued Uncle Fraser.

00:52:53.068 --> 00:52:54.900
Then we have a party.

00:52:54.960 --> 00:52:56.220
Said Ben.

00:52:56.281 --> 00:52:58.141
My uncle smiled.

00:52:58.201 --> 00:53:00.140
A fine party.

00:53:00.200 --> 00:53:01.301
He said.

00:53:01.361 --> 00:53:04.112
We sing and dance until morning.

00:53:04.172 --> 00:53:06.232
Do come, Megan.

00:53:06.292 --> 00:53:08.173
I looked at them.

00:53:08.233 --> 00:53:12.034
Hug Manet on Skye and Betty McCallister's party.

00:53:12.094 --> 00:53:16.776
Singing songs round the piano with Uncle Fraser, Ben and Mum.

00:53:16.836 --> 00:53:20.828
I wanted this more than anything else in the world.

00:53:20.888 --> 00:53:25.659
Yes, I said, and I hugged my uncle again.

00:53:25.719 --> 00:53:28.042
Then it was time to go.

00:53:28.102 --> 00:53:41.068
We rode in the trap to Armadale, and when we passed the ruined castle, I thought I saw a silvery white horse arching its neck and galloping in the waves.

00:53:41.128 --> 00:53:51.097
Good-bye, Kendra, I whispered, and on the salty air there came a cry, as the little white horse called out to me.

00:53:51.157 --> 00:53:54.924
Of course, we knew this was not good-bye.

